---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const life = [
  ['11 May 1912', 'Born at Berlin', 'To Auguste Luwinski, nineteen and unmarried. He took her surname and kept it for life.', 'doc'],
  ['5 Jan 1924', 'His parents marry', 'Auguste Luwinski and Jacob Leibholz, at Berlin-Schöneberg — twelve years after his birth.', 'doc'],
  ['5 Aug 1925', 'A brother', 'Siegfried Leibholz, born at Martin-Luther-Straße 60, Berlin-Schöneberg. Same parents; the other surname.', 'doc'],
  ['1933', 'Emigrates to Johannesburg', 'Aged twenty-one, in the year the Nazis took power.', 'doc'],
  ['from 1933', 'South Africa', 'Occupation recorded as dress designer — his mother was a Schneiderin und Modistin.', 'doc'],
  ['26 Feb 1943', 'His father deported', 'Jacob Leibholz, from Berlin to Auschwitz. Kurt had been ten years gone.', 'doc'],
  ['19 Jun 1948', 'Marries Ivone', 'Johannesburg, certificate 858, by special licence. He is 36, a BACHELOR, born Berlin, occupation shop keeper. She is 24, born Portuguese East Africa, and DIVORCED. Married WITH antenuptial contract, on special licence 164531 taken out ten days before, by J.J. Greyling, Magistrate. Both at 108 Dunottar Street, Sydenham.', 'doc'],
  ['1 Nov 1941', 'Buys the Piccadilly Hat Company', 'Hannah Court, 81 Eloff Street, Johannesburg, with Walter Oppenheimer, from Ivy Fuchs. Sole owner from 31 October 1942; still trading there in 1952.', 'doc'],
  ['after Jun 1948', 'Files for divorce', 'Within weeks of the wedding, and before his son was born.', 'doc'],
  ['1951', 'Divorce', 'Illiquid case, restitution of conjugal rights: Kurt Luwinski versus Ivone, formerly de Witt, born Figueiredo.', 'doc'],
  ['29 Feb 1956', 'Marries Frances Hostowsky', 'Registered in the Hanover, Orange Free State records.', 'doc'],
  ['1957', 'Files for divorce again', 'Kurt Luwinski versus Frances, formerly Sefton, formerly Berner, born Hostowsky.', 'doc'],
  ['1957', 'And the bank sues him', 'Standard Bank of South Africa Ltd versus K Luwinski — Witwatersrand Local Division, 2542/1957. Two actions in one year.', 'doc'],
  ['15 Dec 1968', 'Dies at Johannesburg', 'Aged 56. Buried the next day: Westpark, block L, grave 211. Estate file 7584/1969 follows in the new year.', 'doc'],
  ['16 Dec 1968', 'Buried Westpark, Johannesburg', 'By the Chevrah Kadisha. Yahrzeit 24 Kislev 5729. He was buried as a Jew.', 'doc'],
];
---
<Base title="Kurt Israel Luwinski"
  subtitle="Berlin, 11 May 1912 — Johannesburg, 15 December 1968"
  lede="He was born with his mother's name because his parents would not marry for another twelve years. He left Germany at twenty-one and never lived there again. His father, his uncles and his cousins were killed while he was cutting dresses eight thousand kilometres away.">

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th></th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {life.map(([when, what, detail, tag]) => (
        <tr><td>{when}</td><td><strong>{what}</strong></td><td>{detail}</td><td><span class={'tag ' + tag}>Documented</span></td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>1933</h2>
  <p>
    The tree records his emigration in a single word and a year: <em>Johannesburg, Süd Afrika, 1933</em>.
    He was twenty-one. His father was a Kaufmann in Schöneberg; his mother a dressmaker from an East
    Prussian farming family. He arrived in a city with an established German-Jewish trade and took up
    a needle trade of his own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ten years later, on 26 February 1943, his father was put on a train from Berlin to Auschwitz — the
    day before the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>, the round-up of the last Jewish forced labourers in the city.
    Within six weeks his uncle Samuel was dead at Auschwitz too. Nothing in the record says what Kurt
    knew, or when.
  </p>

  <h2>The stone</h2>
  <p>
    Westpark Cemetery, block L, grave 211. Photographed by the burial society, and it carries more
    than his dates.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    אִישׁ יָקָר וְחָבִיב<br />
    <strong>ר׳ לייב קלמן בר דוד</strong><br />
    נפטר כ״ד כסלו תשכ״ט<br />
    תנצב״ה
    <br /><br />
    IN CHERISHED MEMORY OF MY DEAR HUSBAND<br />
    <strong>(LU) KURT LUWINSKI</strong><br />
    WHO PASSED AWAY <strong>15TH DECEMBER 1968</strong>, <strong>AGE 56</strong><br />
    ALWAYS REMEMBERED BY HIS LOVING <strong>WIFE PAULA</strong>, <strong>SON</strong>,
    <strong>MOTHER</strong> AND FRIENDS
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <em>“A dear and beloved man, R' Leib Kalman bar David, died 24 Kislev 5729.”</em> Pauline — Paula
    — put it up. <strong>His mother was alive</strong> and is named on it; Auguste outlived him by
    seven years. <strong>“Son”</strong> is singular.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And his father's name is not Jacob <span class="tag doc">Documented reading</span></h3>
    <p>
      A <em>matzevah</em> names the father. Jacob Leibholz would ordinarily be
      <strong>יעקב — Yaakov</strong>. The stone says <strong>בר דוד</strong>: <strong>bar David</strong>,
      son of David.
    </p>
    <p>Three explanations fit, and one photograph will not choose between them:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Jacob's Hebrew name was David.</strong> German Jews often carried a civil name and a Hebrew name that were not translations of each other. Ordinary, though not the default.</li>
      <li><strong>Kurt's father was a man called David</strong> — and Jacob, who married Auguste twelve years after Kurt was born and is named as his father only in the family record, was not.</li>
      <li><strong>Paula supplied the name</strong>, in Johannesburg in 1968, for a father-in-law she never met who had been dead a quarter of a century in a country she had probably never seen.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small">
      The second would be a second paternity break, one generation above the one this archive
      already found. The third is the dullest and, on this evidence, no less likely.
      <span class="tag inf">Open</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A further oddity</h3>
    <p>
      His mother was a Catholic East Prussian, so by halakha Kurt was <strong>not Jewish</strong> —
      and he was nonetheless buried by the Chevrah Kadisha, under <em>Reb</em>, with a Hebrew name in
      the standard <em>ben</em> form. A convert is normally written <em>ben Avraham</em>; he is not.
      Either the community did not apply the test, or something about his mother is not what the
      family record says.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The name</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Where does “Israel” come from? <span class="tag inf">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      From 1 January 1939 the Nazi state compelled German Jewish men whose first names were not on an
      approved list to insert <em>Israel</em>, and women <em>Sara</em>. It is why the tree also carries
      a “Samuel <em>Israel</em> Leibholz”.
    </p>
    <p>
      For his uncle, the document has been found. <strong>Yad Vashem's <em>List of Jews murdered in
      Auschwitz</em> records him as “Samuel <em>Israel</em> Leibholz”</strong>, born Schubin — the camp
      registers used the names the German state had forced on people. The Bundesarchiv, working from
      civil sources, has him as plain “Samuel”. Both are right; they copy different documents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kurt is the one that still doesn't fit — <strong>or rather, half of what did not fit has now been
      settled, and the half that remains is a different question.</strong> He had been in South Africa
      for six years by 1939, and as the son of a Jewish father and a Catholic East Prussian mother he
      would ordinarily have been <em>Mischling ersten Grades</em>, to whom the name decree did not apply.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The decree itself, and it reaches across an ocean <span class="tag doc">17 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      The law has been read rather than paraphrased. It is the <strong>Zweite Verordnung zur Durchführung
      des Gesetzes über die Änderung von Familiennamen und Vornamen</strong>, of <strong>17 August
      1938</strong>, promulgated the following day — <em>Deutsches Reichsgesetzblatt</em> 1938,
      <strong>Teil I, Nr. 130, Seite 1044</strong>, signed by Stuckart for the Interior Ministry and by
      Gürtner for Justice. The page is below. It is a published German law and therefore in the public
      domain.
    </p>

    <figure class="doc-scan">
      <img src={base + '/rgbl-1938-1044.png'} width="1024" height="1460" loading="lazy"
           alt="Page 1044 of the Reichsgesetzblatt, Part I, 1938, carrying the Second Decree on the Alteration of Family Names and Forenames of 17 August 1938." />
      <figcaption class="small muted">
        <em>Reichsgesetzblatt</em> I, 1938, p. 1044. Scan via Wikimedia Commons from the Austrian
        National Library's digitised run of the <em>Reichsgesetzblatt</em>. Public domain as a published
        German law.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p><strong>§ 2 (1) is the clause everybody knows.</strong></p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="de">
        „Soweit Juden andere Vornamen führen, als sie nach § 1 Juden beigelegt werden dürfen, müssen sie
        vom 1. Januar 1939 ab zusätzlich einen weiteren Vornamen annehmen, und zwar männliche Personen
        den Vornamen <strong>Israel</strong>, weibliche Personen den Vornamen <strong>Sara</strong>.“
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Where Jews bear forenames other than those which may be given to Jews under § 1, they must from
        1 January 1939 additionally adopt a further forename — male persons the forename
        <strong>Israel</strong>, female persons the forename <strong>Sara</strong>.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      <strong>§ 2 (3) is the clause that answers Kurt, and this archive did not know it was there.</strong>
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="de">
        „<strong>Hat der Anzeigepflichtige seinen Wohnsitz oder gewöhnlichen Aufenthalt im Ausland</strong>,
        so ist die im Abs. 2 Satz 1 vorgesehene Anzeige an Stelle der Ortspolizeibehörde
        <strong>dem zuständigen deutschen Konsul</strong> zu erstatten.“
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Where the person obliged to notify has his domicile or habitual residence <strong>abroad</strong>,
        the notification required by paragraph 2, sentence 1 is to be made to the competent
        <strong>German consul</strong> in place of the local police authority.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      <strong>So &ldquo;he had already left&rdquo; was never an objection.</strong> The decree was written
      with emigrants in mind and says so in its own text: it names the consulate as the office you report
      to, and it routes births and marriages registered abroad to <em>Standesamt I</em> in Berlin. A man
      in Johannesburg in 1939 was inside its reach, and the machinery for reaching him was specified.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And § 1 (2) closes the other door.</strong> The decree does not apply to
      <em>„Juden, die eine fremde Staatsangehörigkeit besitzen&ldquo;</em> — Jews holding foreign
      citizenship. <strong>Kurt held none.</strong> This archive established separately, and for other
      reasons, that he never naturalised: he arrived in 1933, was an alien for thirty-five years and
      died one in 1968. The exemption that would have released him is the exact thing he did not have.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>What this does and does not prove.</strong> It removes the geography as an objection and it
      removes the citizenship exemption. It does <em>not</em> establish that Kurt was classed as a
      <em>Jude</em> rather than a <em>Mischling</em> — that is the question below, and the decree is
      silent on it because it simply says <em>Juden</em> and leaves the definition to the Reich Citizenship
      Law. Nor is there a document here showing that Kurt personally notified a consul. What there is: a
      law that reached South Africa, a man it was not exempt from, and the name on his records.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And he is in the Arolsen Archives, which nobody expected <span class="tag inf">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      The <a href={base + '/leibholz/'}>Arolsen Archives</a> — the international centre on Nazi
      persecution — were searched for the first time on 17 September 2026. Among nine Luwinski entries
      is one that stops you:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p><strong>LUWINSKI, Kurt — 11.05.1912</strong><br />
      <span class="small">DocID 11214358 · <em>1 Incarceration Documents / 1.2 Miscellaneous /
      1.2.1 Deportations and Transports / Part 24: Folder &ldquo;MFN01335&rdquo;</em> ·
      reference 10000375 24 · five hundred documents</span></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>That is his date of birth to the day.</strong> And <strong>Kurt was not deported</strong>
      — he left Germany in 1933, ten years before his father&rsquo;s transport, and died in Johannesburg
      in 1968 in a bed. So what is he doing in a folder of deportations and transports?
    </p>
    <p>
      That guess was written this morning and it was only a guess: that he must be named
      <em>inside</em> a document about somebody else. <strong>By the afternoon it had evidence.</strong>
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p><strong>LEIBHOLZ, Jacob</strong> — b. 08.08.1884 — <strong>DocID 11214358</strong><br />
      <strong>LEIBHOLZ, Auguste</strong>, <em>née LUWWINSKY</em> — b. 28.08.1893 — <strong>DocID 11214358</strong><br />
      <strong>LUWINSKI, Kurt</strong> — b. 11.05.1912 — <strong>DocID 11214358</strong></p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      <strong>It is one document.</strong> The father who was deported, the mother who was not, and the
      son who had been gone ten years — <strong>all three named on a single sheet</strong>, in a folder
      of deportations and transports.
    </p>
    <p>
      Neither Kurt nor his mother was deported. He was in Johannesburg from 1933 and died there in 1968;
      <strong>Auguste survived the war in Berlin</strong> and lived until 1975. A deportation form that
      records a man&rsquo;s household and his next of kin is exactly the kind of paper that carries a
      wife and an absent son. <strong>The somebody else is his father.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>What is still inference, and it has to stay labelled.</strong> The shared DocID is a hard
      fact from the index. <em>What kind of document it is</em> is not. It could be a transport list, a
      property declaration, a Gestapo household record, or something assembled after the war. Nobody
      here has read it. Three times in the last fortnight this archive has taken a good fit for a
      finding and had to withdraw it in public, so the distinction is being kept even where the reading
      now looks obvious.
    </p>
    <p class="verdict disp">
      <strong>The document was not opened, and the reason is a rule and not an obstacle.</strong>
      Arolsen requires a <em>declaration of consent</em> to the use of sensitive personal data before a
      document can be read. That is a statement a named person makes about their own conduct, and
      <strong>I do not make it on David&rsquo;s behalf</strong>. The index needs no consent, and
      everything above came from it. <strong>If one file in this whole archive is ever opened, it should
      be this one</strong> — it is the only wartime German record known to carry Kurt&rsquo;s name, and
      it carries his father&rsquo;s and his mother&rsquo;s on the same page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the citizenship question has the same shape <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      The obvious move is to search the <em>Ausbürgerungslisten</em> — the denaturalisations published
      by name in the <em>Reichsanzeiger</em>. <strong>It is the wrong door.</strong> Those were
      individual cases, about 39,000 of them across 359 lists, and they were aimed at politicians,
      writers and public figures. A twenty-one-year-old dress designer who left quietly in 1933 would
      never have appeared on one, so <strong>his absence from them means nothing</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If Kurt lost his German citizenship, he lost it on <strong>25 November 1941</strong>, under the
      <strong>11th Decree to the Reich Citizenship Law</strong>, which stripped it automatically and
      collectively from <strong>every German Jew living outside the Reich</strong> — between 250,000
      and 280,000 people — and confiscated their property at the same stroke. No proceedings. No
      names published anywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>But the 11th Decree applied to <em>Juden</em>, and so did the name.</strong> A
      <em>Mischling</em> enrolled in the Jewish religious community was reclassified a
      <strong>Geltungsjude</strong> — counted as a Jew — and then all of it applies at once: the
      <em>Israel</em>, the 1941 statelessness, and a Jewish burial in Johannesburg thirty years later
      with a Hebrew name in the ordinary form.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      That is the reading which makes four separate facts agree, instead of leaving three of them as
      anomalies. The <strong>Berlin Gemeinde membership registers</strong> would settle it, and so
      would the <strong>1939 census supplementary card</strong> for Meraner Straße — which asked this
      family, in writing, how many Jewish grandparents each of them had.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Piccadilly Hat Company, 81 Eloff Street <span class="tag doc">New, 14 September 2026</span></h2>
  <p>
    This archive had Kurt landing in Johannesburg in 1933 and then <strong>nothing at all</strong>
    until his marriage in June 1948. Fifteen years, blank. A page of the
    <em>Government Gazette of the Union of South Africa</em> closed most of them, and the source it
    came from closed the rest.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What the gazettes say</h3>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Gazette</th><th>Entry</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>8 Nov 1940</td><td><a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1940-11-08/2825/eng@1940-11-08#page-67">2825, p.67</a></td><td><em>“Notice of sale of business. The business of <strong>Piccadilly Hat</strong> …”</em> — an earlier sale of the same shop</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>7, 14, 21 Nov 1941</strong></td><td><a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1941-11-07/2960/eng@1941-11-07#page-75">2960</a> · <a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1941-11-14/2961/eng@1941-11-14#page-72">2961</a> · <a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1941-11-21/2963/eng@1941-11-21#page-66">2963</a></td><td><em>“The <strong>Piccadilly Hat Company</strong> carried on by <strong>Ivy Fuchs</strong>, at <strong>Hannah Court, 81 Eloff Street</strong> … transferred as from <strong>1st November 1941</strong> to <strong>Walter Oppenheimer and Kurt Luwinski</strong>”</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>16, 23, 30 Oct 1942</strong></td><td><a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1942-10-16/3104/eng@1942-10-16#page-53">3104</a> · <a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1942-10-23/3106/eng@1942-10-23#page-45">3106</a> · <a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1942-10-30/3110/eng@1942-10-30#page-43">3110</a></td><td><em>“The partnership hitherto existing between Walter Oppenheimer and Kurt Luwinski, trading as Piccadilly Hat Company, at 81 Eloff Street, Johannesburg, will be dissolved as from the <strong>31st October, 1942</strong>, and the business will be taken over with all assets and liabilities and continued under the same style and at the same address by <strong>Kurt Luwinski</strong>.”</em> Attorney K. Steinberg, Howard House</td></tr>
        <tr><td>28 Apr 1939</td><td><a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1939-04-28/2630/eng@1939-04-28#page-67">2630, p.67</a></td><td>Estate <strong>1107/39, “Joseph Fuchs and surviving spouse, Ivy Fuchs”</strong> — she is executrix, of 64 Twist Street. <strong>The woman who sold them the shop was a widow</strong>, two years bereaved, and she sold it to two refugees</td></tr>
        <tr><td>16 May 1952</td><td><a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1952-05-16/4846/eng@1952-05-16#page-93">4846, p.93</a></td><td><strong>“Piccadilly Hat Co. … 81 Eloff Street, Johannesburg”</strong> — still trading, eleven years on</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10 Nov 1972</td><td><a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1972-11-10/3701/eng@1972-11-10#page-126">3701, p.126</a></td><td>Sequestration, estate <strong>T1301/72</strong>: <em>Phillip Crystal, a commercial traveller, Mark Hotel, O&rsquo;Reilly Road, Berea</em> &mdash; order 17/10/72, Witwatersrand Local &mdash; <strong>upon the application of Paula Luwinski</strong> <span class="tag disp">Corrected</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>25 Jul 1975</td><td><a class="src" href="https://gazettes.africa/akn/za/officialGazette/government-gazette/1975-07-25/4801/eng@1975-07-25#page-95">4801, p.95</a></td><td><strong>Paula Luwinski · Pallou's Dress Centre, 18 Wanderers Street, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Every gazette reference above is a link to the page itself.</strong> Each was checked on
      15 September 2026 and each resolves; the page number is in the anchor, so the link lands on the
      notice rather than on the front of a three-hundred-page issue.
    </p>
    <p>
      So he <strong>bought a hat shop on Johannesburg's main shopping street on 1 November 1941</strong>,
      ran it in partnership for exactly one year to the day, took it over alone, and was still there in
      1952. <strong>And it explains “Shop Keeper”</strong> — the occupation on the 1948 marriage register
      that sat so oddly beside <em>dress designer</em> in 1933 and again in 1956. It is the same man in
      the same trade throughout; in 1948 he simply owned the shop.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A correction to that table <span class="tag disp">Corrected 15 September</span></h3>
    <p>
      The 1972 line used to read here <em>&ldquo;Paula Luwinski, a commercial traveller, Mark
      Hotel&rdquo;</em>. That came from a <strong>search snippet</strong>, and a snippet flattens a
      four-column table into one string. The gazette itself, read at layout, prints its own headings:
      <em>No. of estate/company</em> &middot; <em>Name and description of estate/company</em> &middot;
      <em>Date upon which and division of court by which order made</em> &middot;
      <strong>Upon the application of</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      So <strong>Phillip Crystal</strong> is the commercial traveller at the Mark Hotel, and he is the
      man being sequestrated. <strong>Paula Luwinski is the creditor who brought it.</strong> Reading
      the page also yields what the snippet never had: the estate number <strong>T1301/72</strong> and
      the date of the order, <strong>17 October 1972</strong>, Witwatersrand Local Division &mdash;
      which matches the court file this archive already held, <strong>WLD 2451/1972</strong>,
      <em>Paula Luwinski v. Phillip Crystal</em>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      The archive had these two people the right way round on one page and the wrong way round on
      another, because one entry was read from a document and the other from a snippet. It is the
      Caroline Walls mistake in a different costume.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>One trade, five people, three countries <span class="tag inf">Now documented at every point</span></h3>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Auguste</strong>, his mother</td><td>dressmaker and <strong>milliner</strong>, East Prussia</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Kurt</strong></td><td>dress designer 1933 · <strong>hats, 81 Eloff Street, 1941–52</strong> · shop keeper 1948 · dress designer 1956</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ivone</strong>, second wife</td><td><strong>dressmaker</strong>, firm — February 1948</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Frances</strong>, third wife</td><td><strong>buyer</strong>, firm — 1956</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Pauline</strong>, his widow</td><td>sequestrating creditor 1972 · <strong>Pallou's Dress Centre</strong>, 18 Wanderers Street, 1975</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="small">
      This archive used to say, carefully, that two occupations on a certificate were <em>the first
      plausible account of how Kurt and Ivone met</em>. It can stop being careful. <strong>He owned the
      shop.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>His partner naturalised. He did not. <span class="tag open">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      The Union gazette published applications for naturalisation under the <strong>Aliens Act of
      1926</strong>, and <strong>Walter Oppenheimer made one in February 1942</strong> — notice
      <strong>1462</strong>, gazette <strong>3007</strong> of 20 February 1942, page 115. Eight months
      later the partnership dissolved.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Kurt appears in none of them.</strong> Searching all 54,313 South African issues returns
      exactly eight Luwinski documents, and not one is a naturalisation notice — under either spelling,
      and the optical character recognition demonstrably does find him, mangled, elsewhere.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      That is a negative and it is stated as one: a name can be lost in a dense printed list, and the
      corpus may not hold every issue. But a naturalisation notice gives <strong>date and place of birth
      over an official signature</strong>, which is exactly what
      <a href={base + '/open-questions/'}>question 4</a> — where the compulsory <em>Israel</em> came
      from — has always lacked. If he never naturalised, that is itself worth knowing about a man who
      arrived in 1933 and died there in 1968.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the date is hard to read without flinching</h3>
    <p>
      The partnership dissolved on <strong>31 October 1942</strong>. His uncle <strong>Julius Leibke</strong>
      had died in the Jewish Hospital on Iranische Straße in Berlin on <strong>12 October 1942</strong>,
      nineteen days earlier. His father <strong>Jacob Leibholz</strong> was deported to Auschwitz on
      <strong>26 February 1943</strong>, four months later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kurt was taking sole ownership of a hat shop on Eloff Street in the same season his family was
      being destroyed in Berlin. Both facts are documented, and the archive sets them beside each other
      without adding anything to them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Three wives, two divorces filed</h2>
  <p>
    Ivone in 1948, divorced 1951. Frances Hostowsky in 1956, divorce filed 1957. Pauline — recorded in
    the tree under the names Rappoport and Miller, born 29 September 1914, died of pancreatic cancer on
    17 November 1985 — appears as his wife without a date, and outlived him by sixteen years. Both
    divorce actions are “illiquid case, restitution of conjugal rights”, which in South African practice
    of the period was the standard first step towards a decree, not a reconciliation.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The second wedding, read in full <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      It was in the family tree the whole time, filed as a photograph and titled
      <em>record-image_</em>, attached not to Kurt but to Frances. The thumbnail is unreadable. The
      original scan is <strong>3,918 × 5,245 pixels</strong>, and at that size every field on it can
      be read. Form <strong>B.M.D. 3 (a)</strong>, entry <strong>No. 169 of 1956</strong>: the
      <em>duplicate original register</em> of Kurt's second marriage. That is the second time in this
      archive that a marriage register has been sitting in the tree disguised as a family snapshot.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Register entry No. 169, 1956</th><th>Husband</th><th>Wife</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Names</td><td><strong>LUWINSKI, Kurt</strong></td><td><strong>SEFTON; formerly Berner, born Hostovsky — Frances</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Date and place</td><td colspan="2"><strong>Twenty Ninth February 1956</strong>, Johannesburg, district Johannesburg, Transvaal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Identity number</td><td><strong>331/398110</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Age and date of birth</td><td>43 yrs <strong>(11:5:1912)</strong></td><td>41 yrs <strong>(19:1:1915)</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Personal status</td><td><strong>Divorcee</strong></td><td><strong>Widow</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Occupation</td><td><strong>Dress designer</strong>, private</td><td><strong>Buyer</strong>, firm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Place of birth</td><td><strong>Berlin, Germany</strong></td><td><strong>Vienna, Austria</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Address at marriage</td><td colspan="2"><strong>1 Houghton Crest, Sharp Street, Bellevue, Johannesburg</strong> — both of them</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Licence</td><td colspan="2">Special licence <strong>No. 265004, dated 29/2/56</strong> — the day of the wedding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Contract</td><td colspan="2"><strong>With ante-nuptial contract</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Remarks</td><td colspan="2"><strong>“Order filed. Affidavit filed. Birth certificates not available.”</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Signed by</td><td colspan="2">K. Luwinski and Frances Sefton, before <strong>G. Rosenblatt</strong> and <strong>Walter Herz</strong>; marriage officer <strong>Alfred Reinach</strong>, Johannesburg</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>The word that matters is <em>Divorcee</em>.</strong> Until now the end of the marriage to
      Ivone rested on a single line in the national archives index — a 1951 illiquid case. Here a
      registrar records Kurt's status as divorced and notes an <strong>order filed</strong>: he had to
      put the decree on the table before he could be married again, and the registrar saw it. The
      divorce is confirmed from the other side.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And the parents' columns are ruled through.</strong> This form, unlike the 1948 one, does
      have a section C for the particulars of the parents of the husband and of the wife. The officer
      drew a diagonal line across it and left it empty. The reason is written four lines above:
      <em>birth certificates not available</em>. Neither a Berlin Jew who left in 1933 nor a Viennese
      woman born in 1915 could produce one, so he took an affidavit instead. The register that could
      have named <strong>Jacob Leibholz</strong> in Kurt's own lifetime has the column, and still says
      nothing. <span class="tag open">Closes a hope</span>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Two by-products worth chasing. The <strong>identity number 331/398110</strong> is a searchable key
      for estate, deeds and naturalisation files — the same Johannesburg 331 prefix carried by
      Nuno Lerena's number in the sibling archive. And an <strong>ante-nuptial contract</strong> executed
      on or just before 29 February 1956 is registered in the Johannesburg Deeds Office.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>And he did it the first time too.</strong> Re-reading the June 1948 register at full
      resolution corrected column 10 from <em>without</em> to <strong>With A.N.C.</strong>
      <span class="tag disp">Self-corrected</span> So there are <strong>two ante-nuptial contracts</strong>
      in the Johannesburg Deeds Office — one from June 1948, one from February 1956. The 1948 one was
      drawn up in the month Derrick's parents married, the year before he was born, and unlike a
      marriage register it is not confined to an eleven-column form. It is the best remaining chance
      of a 1948 document that says something about who Ivone was.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The same action, twice <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      Married Ivone 19 June 1948, sued her for <strong>restitution of conjugal rights</strong> in 1951.
      Married Frances 29 February 1956, sued her for <strong>restitution of conjugal rights</strong> in
      1957. Frances remarried — Rolf Eisenhardt — on 23 May 1958. The same instrument, twice, against
      two wives, six years apart.
    </p>
    <p>
      In South African practice of the period that action was the compulsory first step to a decree on
      the ground of <em>desertion</em>: you asked the court to order her to return, and when she did not,
      the order converted to a divorce. It is the legal signature of a wife who has left. Both of
      Kurt's marriages ended that way, and there was a small child in the first of them.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Frances carried five surnames in one life: <strong>Hostovsky</strong> by birth, then
      <strong>Berner</strong>, <strong>Sefton</strong>, <strong>Luwinski</strong>,
      <strong>Eisenhardt</strong>. The 1956 register gives her birthplace as <strong>Vienna</strong>;
      the family tree had only “Austria”. In 1956 she was a widow and a buyer for a firm — on that
      document she is the one of the two with an employer.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>He died in 1968, not 1969 <span class="tag doc">Corrected</span></h3>
    <p>
      The family tree gives 15 December <strong>1969</strong>, and this archive repeated it. The
      Johannesburg Chevrah Kadisha burial register gives <strong>15 December 1968</strong> — and the
      Hebrew date settles it independently: <strong>24 Kislev 5729</strong> is 15 December 1968. Two
      datings of the same day, agreeing with each other and not with the tree.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="tag disp">Withdrawn 15 September 2026</strong>
      <s>And the estate notice has now been found. Government Gazette 2525 of 26 September 1969,
      page 109, carries estate 7584/69, wound up from the <em>Boedelafdeling</em> at Sanlam Building
      806, 63 Commissioner Street, Johannesburg &mdash; Sanlam Trust.</s>
      <strong>It is not Kurt&rsquo;s notice.</strong> The gazette was downloaded and read in full on
      15 September. <strong>The word &ldquo;Luwinski&rdquo; does not appear in it once.</strong> What
      is on that page, in a dense two-column list of estate notices, is this:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="small">
      <strong>8923/69 &mdash; ROBBERTZE, Alida Christina</strong>, gebore Bezuidenhout &hellip; Die
      Trust Bank van Afrika Bpk., Trust- en <strong>Boedelafdeling, Sanlamgebou 806,
      Commissionerstraat 63</strong>, Johannesburg.<br />
      <strong>7584/69 &mdash; KOTZE, Pieter Johannes</strong>, gebore op 11 Januarie 1904, van
      Parklaan 8, Bethal-stasie, Bethal &hellip; Boedelafdeling, Pretoriusstraat 286, Pretoria.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>Two adjacent notices, and this archive took the estate number from one and the address
      from the other.</strong> Neither belongs to Kurt. <em>Sanlamgebou 806</em> appears exactly once
      in the whole issue, and it is Alida Robbertze&rsquo;s.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>So Kurt&rsquo;s estate notice has not been found</strong>, and the archive is back where
      it was: the estate is known from the National Archives index, <strong>TAB MHG 7584/69,
      &ldquo;Luwinski, Kurt&rdquo;</strong>, and from nothing else. One loose end is left showing
      rather than tidied: the gazette prints <em>7584/69</em> against a Bethal man wound up from
      Pretoria, and a Transvaal estate number cannot belong to two people. Either the OCR has misread
      a digit on a small-print number, or one of the two references is wrong. <strong>Unresolved, and
      recorded as unresolved.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>How it happened, because it is worth saying.</strong> The notice was matched on an
      estate <em>number</em> alone, across an OCR&rsquo;d two-column page, without reading the name
      beside it. That is the same mistake as the Paula Luwinski line on this very page &mdash; a
      fragment taken for a document. Twice in one week, from the same cause: <strong>trusting a search
      hit instead of opening the page.</strong>
    <p>
      It also makes the estate file read naturally instead of oddly. <strong>MHG 7584/69</strong> is
      an estate opened in 1969 on a death the previous December, which is ordinary; an estate opened
      in the same month would have been the strange thing.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>So Kurt was 56 when he died, not 57.</strong>
      He lies at Westpark, <strong>block L, grave 211</strong>. Pauline was buried twenty-six years
      later in block V, grave 1470, still under his name. There is no family plot.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The national archives settle the end of it. His estate file — <strong>Transvaal, Master of the High
    Court, 7584/69, “Luwinski, Kurt”</strong> — carries the remark <em>“surviving spouse Pauline
    Luwinski”</em>. So Pauline was his widow, and the undated marriage in the family tree was his last.
    <span class="tag doc">Documented</span> The same index shows Frances remarrying in 1958, the year
    after he sued her: <strong>Rolf Eisenhardt and Frances Luwinski</strong>, formerly Sefton, formerly
    Berner, born Hostowski, in the Cape Town and Wynberg marriage register.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And that register was in the tree too <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      Photo 9500005, 2,495 × 3,119, titled only <em>image</em>, never opened. Entry
      <strong>No. 183 of 1958</strong>, Wynberg, Cape: <strong>Rolf Eisenhardt</strong> and
      <strong>Frances Luwinski, formerly Sefton, formerly Berner, born Hostowski</strong>, married
      <strong>23 May 1958</strong>. Her status: <strong>Divorcee</strong>. Column 14:
      <strong>“Decree of Divorce attached to D.O.M.R.”</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Which closes the other end of Kurt's life the way the 1956 register closed the first. Both of
      his divorce actions were <em>granted</em> — Ivone's before February 1956, Frances's before May
      1958 — and in each case the proof is a registrar on the far side of the case, looking at the
      decree and writing that he had seen it. The marriage to Frances lasted about two years.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      It also gives <strong>Rolf Eisenhardt</strong> a life, where the family tree had only a name and
      no dates at all: born <strong>11 December 1931 in Germany</strong>, a <strong>ladies'
      hairdresser</strong>, a bachelor of 26, living at 9 Trianon Court, Avenue Marseille, Sea Point.
      Frances was 43 and managing a restaurant. She had left Johannesburg for the Cape, and she married
      a man seventeen years younger than herself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A Luwinski at number 32 and a Leibholz at number 31</h2>
  <p>
    This archive held one line about the year after Kurt was born: <em>1913, “LuwinSki, I.,
    Invalide.”</em> No street, no house, no district. It had never been opened at the page.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Chodowieckistraße, Berlin N 55 <span class="tag doc">New, 20 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Berliner Adressbuch 1913, Teil III, page 139. At <strong>number 32, on the corner of
      Winsstraße</strong>, among a locksmith, a merchant, a butcher, a joiner, a teacher and a
      furrier: <strong>Luwinski, J., Invalide</strong>. And at <strong>number 31, the house next
      door: Leibholz, G., Kaufmann.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      The initial is <strong>J, not I</strong> — the text layer read a Fraktur J as an I. In Prussian
      usage <em>Invalide</em> is a status, not a complaint: a man on a disability or veteran's pension,
      out of his trade. Joachim Luwinski, born at Allenstein on 21 October 1865, is recorded elsewhere
      as a <em>Landwirt</em>, and would have been forty-seven. <strong>A directory gives an initial,
      not a man</strong>, and nothing here proves it is him.
    </p>
    <p>
      The volume was compiled in the <strong>autumn of 1912</strong>. Kurt was born on 11 May 1912.
      This is where a Luwinski household was in the year of his birth.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>How the street was settled, rather than assumed <span class="tag doc">Method</span></h3>
    <p>
      A running head names the first and last street on a page, not the street a given column belongs
      to. Reading a house number off one column and a street name off the header is precisely the
      two-adjacent-entries error this archive made with the 1969 estate notice, so the heading was
      hunted down instead: it is on the previous page —
      <strong>“NO. 55 · Chodowieckistr. · A.G. Berl. Mitte — L.G. I”</strong> — and the numbers run
      unbroken from 1 through 40 before the street ends at an arrow reading
      <em>← Greifswalder Str. →</em> and a new heading begins, <em>N.37 Choriner Str.</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      And the corner confirms it. Number 32 is marked <em>s. a. Winsstr. 42</em> and number 33
      <em>s. a. Winsstr. 33</em> — consecutive numbers facing each other across a junction, which is
      what Berlin numbering does where one street meets another.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>Eleven years later Auguste Luwinski married Jacob Leibholz.</strong> Nothing here proves
    the two households are those families, and both surnames are borne by other Berliners. What it
    does is give that marriage a geography for the first time — and it gives Kurt's birth a
    <strong>district: Prenzlauer Berg</strong>, which is the thing the search below was missing.
  </p>

  <h2>Standesamt Berlin VIII, 1912, number 963</h2>
  <p>
    This morning Kurt's birth registration was a blank. By the evening it had a citation.
  </p>

  <figure class="doc-scan">
    <img src={base + '/kurt-963.png'} alt="A line from a handwritten alphabetical index: Luwinski, Kurt Myszislaus, 963" />
    <figcaption class="small muted">
      Landesarchiv Berlin, <strong>P Rep. 523 Nr. 1395</strong> — name index to the birth register of
      <strong>Standesamt Berlin VIII, 1912</strong>, page 75, the May block:
      <strong>“Luwinski · Kurt Myszislaus. · 963”</strong>, between Lentz (919) and Labardin (965).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>How the volume was chosen, which is most of the work <span class="tag doc">New, 20 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      The Landesarchiv's indexes are organised by registry office, and nobody knew Kurt's. That made
      <strong>twenty-one candidate volumes</strong>. Schöneberg was tried first, because the family is
      documented there from 1925, and the whole 1912 L section was read — four pages, entries 14 to
      1119, Laband to Lüttke. <strong>No Luwinski.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Then the 1913 directory gave <a href="#">Chodowieckistraße 32</a>. And the Berliner Adressbuch
      turns out to carry, in its second part, an alphabetical street index whose
      <strong>very first column is headed “Standesamt”</strong>. The Chodowieckistraße row:
      houses 1–10 → 8, 11–20 → 8, 21–22 → 10c, <strong>23–32 → 8</strong>.
      Twenty-one volumes became one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Why it is him <span class="tag inf">Four things agree</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>The surname</strong>, which reaches only twenty-three pages in forty-four years of Berlin
      directories. <strong>The forename.</strong> <strong>The month</strong> — he was born on 11 May
      1912. And <strong>the district</strong>, arrived at from a street index rather than from the
      birth record, so it is not the same fact counted twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And the arithmetic checks.</strong> Berlin VIII's 1912 register runs past number 2561 by
      the end of the year, so entry 963 is <strong>37.6 per cent</strong> of the way through it. The
      eleventh of May is <strong>36 per cent</strong> of the way through the year. An index gives a
      month, not a day; the proportion supplies the day's worth of confirmation the index withholds.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every L page of the 1912 index was read, January to December. <strong>There is exactly one
      Luwinski in the entire year at that office.</strong> What is still not proved is that he is Kurt
      Israel Luwinski of Johannesburg — an index gives a surname, a forename, a month and a number.
      The certificate proves it, and the certificate is now orderable.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A second forename nobody has ever seen <span class="tag doc">New</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Kurt Myszislaus.</strong> Not the family tree, not the Arolsen index, not the Berlin
      registration card, not one South African document has ever carried a second forename for him.
      It is a German clerk's spelling of a Polish name — <em>Mieczysław</em>. Whether it came from a
      godparent, a grandfather or a saint's day is not something an index can say.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And what the certificate will contain <span class="tag inf">Why it matters</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>The mother:</strong> Auguste Luwinski, nineteen and unmarried, with her age, her
      occupation and <em>her address on 11 May 1912</em>, which this archive has never had.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The father, or the silence where he should be.</strong> An illegitimate birth in Prussia
      in 1912 names the mother and leaves the father's column empty unless he acknowledged the child
      before the registrar. Either answer is worth having: a name would end the oldest question here,
      and a blank would establish that the paternity of record was attached later — and when.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The marginal notes.</strong> German birth entries accumulate <em>Randvermerke</em> over a
      lifetime: legitimation on a parent's marriage, changes of name, and after 1938 the compulsory
      addition of <em>Israel</em>. Auguste married Jacob Leibholz on 5 January 1924.
      <strong>If Kurt was legitimised then, the note is in that margin</strong> — and it would be the
      document that makes Jacob his father in law as well as in family memory.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The rest of the indexes, and what is still unopened</h2>
  <p>
    The Landesarchiv Berlin publishes the <em>Namensverzeichnisse</em> — the alphabetical name indexes
    to the Berlin registry registers — as free PDFs, office by office and year by year. This archive
    had never opened them. Find the surname in the index, read off the entry number, and the
    certificate can be ordered by an exact citation instead of by a plea.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Schöneberg II, 1911 to 1913, thirty-three megabytes <span class="tag doc">New, 20 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>P Rep. 161 Nr. 713.</strong> Kurt was born on <strong>11 May 1912</strong>, and this is
      the birth index for the district the family is documented in from 1925. Schöneberg I's birth
      indexes stop at 1910 and Schöneberg II begins at 1911, so inside Schöneberg there is no gap and
      no second candidate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The 1912 L section of that volume has now been read, page by page, and there is no
      Luwinski in it.</strong> Entries 14 to 1119, four pages, every surname from Laband to Lüttke:
      Kurt was not registered at Schöneberg II. And the directory has since put a Luwinski household
      in <em>Prenzlauer Berg</em>, which is a different answer to the same question. <strong>But the alternative is finite:</strong>
      twenty more single-year 1912 birth indexes are online for the old Roman-numeral city offices,
      between 6 and 35 MB each. Twenty-one files, about 350 MB, all free, all listed.
      <strong>That is a bounded job, not a search.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      And the same instrument holds the marriage. Jacob married Auguste on 5 January 1924, twelve years
      after Kurt was born and took his mother's name. A German marriage entry names the parents of both
      parties, and where a couple legitimise an existing child <strong>the entry carries a marginal note
      saying so</strong>. Two volumes cover the date: Schöneberg I, marriages 1922–1927, P Rep. 160
      Nr. 1036; and Schöneberg II, marriages 1924–1928, P Rep. 161 Nr. 271.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And a false finding, caught before it was written down <span class="tag disp">Method</span></h3>
    <p>
      This page was about to say that <em>Schöneberg, this family's own district, has no online index
      at all.</em> Three queries returned nothing and a sweep of all 142 registry offices put
      Schöneberg, Neukölln, Köpenick and Weißensee in the empty column.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Every office name with an umlaut returned zero, and every name without one returned
      rows.</strong> Four failures, one shared property. The site's database stores the names
      double-encoded — the stored string is literally <code>Standesamt KÃ¶penick</code> — and the match
      is on the whole string, so the <em>correct</em> spelling finds nothing and the mangled one finds
      seventeen volumes. Schöneberg turns out to have more online than almost anywhere: births from
      1874 to 1913, and deaths year by year to 1994.
    </p>
    <p>
      What caught it was not cleverness. It was noticing that all four failures shared a character and
      none of the successes did. <strong>When a set of negatives has something in common, suspect the
      thing they have in common before believing the negatives.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The one file that would let him speak for himself</h2>
  <p>
    Everything on this page is someone else's paper about Kurt: a gazette notice, a burial register,
    a marriage entry, a court index. <strong>There is one kind of document in which he would be the
    one talking</strong> — a compensation file under the Bundesentschädigungsgesetz. An
    <em>Entschädigungsakte</em> holds the applicant's own sworn account of what he lost and when he
    left, his Berlin addresses, his trade, his schooling, what became of his parents, his foreign
    address at the time of filing, and usually affidavits from people who knew him. A man who left
    Germany in 1933 and lived thirty-six years abroad is exactly the profile of a claimant.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And they moved four and a half months ago <span class="tag doc">New, 20 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      This archive's own plan put these files at the Landesarchiv Berlin in B Rep. 025. They were not
      there. Until this year they belonged to the <strong>Entschädigungsbehörde</strong>, a department
      of LABO — a live administrative authority, not an archive. On <strong>1 May 2026 the
      Landesarchiv Berlin took over the whole of it: about 280,000 proceedings, fifteen kilometres of
      paper</strong>, stem files and subsidiary files together. About 860 still-open cases stayed
      behind.
    </p>
    <p>
      The stated minimum for an enquiry is a name and <em>life dates</em>. This archive has those for
      three of them — Kurt, 11 May 1912 to 15 December 1968; Auguste, 28 August 1893 to 22 June 1975;
      Siegfried, born 5 August 1925. <strong>The threshold is met without any further work.</strong>
      The <a href={base + '/letters/'}>letter is drafted</a>, as a supplement to the thread already
      open with the Landesarchiv rather than as a second file.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Ten files a year, free — and a status this archive will not claim <span class="tag inf">Stated, not assumed</span></h3>
    <p>
      The Landesarchiv's page says relatives of Nazi-persecuted people, or those they authorise, may
      have up to ten files a year digitised at no charge, and that the quota covers restitution and
      detention files too.
    </p>
    <p>
      The letter mentions it and <strong>does not assert it</strong>. Derrick is Kurt's son on every
      document there is, and this archive has published at length the
      <a href={base + '/dna/'}>DNA result that puts that paternity in doubt</a>. The German text says
      so plainly and leaves the decision to them. An archive that has questioned a relationship in
      public does not quietly rely on it in private.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And a national index that comes before Berlin <span class="tag doc">New</span></h3>
    <p>
      Named on the Landesarchiv's own page: the <strong>Bundeszentralkartei</strong>, a central index
      of compensation claims for the whole Federal Republic, held by the Bezirksregierung Düsseldorf.
      It matters because § 185 BEG gave each western state its own authority, and this family
      scattered across three jurisdictions — Kurt to Johannesburg, his mother to Baden-Württemberg,
      his brother to Potsdam and the far side of the wall. <strong>A Berlin-only enquiry can come back
      empty while the file sits in Stuttgart.</strong> One letter to the BZK asks the prior question
      for all four of them: did they ever file, and where.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>His brother, who stayed</h2>
  <p>
    Siegfried Leibholz was born at Martin-Luther-Straße 60 in Schöneberg on 5 August 1925 — the same
    parents, the father's surname. The residence record is the part that stops you: from
    <strong>January 1943 to 30 April 1945</strong> he was at Sommerfeld in the Osthavelland, where his
    mother's people farmed. His father was deported from Berlin on 26 February 1943. The boy was moved
    out of the city in the weeks before it happened, and stayed out until the war ended.
  </p>
  <p>
    He joined the Berlin Kreispolizei on 15 February 1947, married Edith Charlotte Zelenka, had a son
    Michael Kurt in 1951 — named, at least in part, for the brother in Johannesburg — and died at
    Potsdam on 1 February 2005. He is buried at the Südwestkirchhof, Stahnsdorf.
  </p>

